This is an iterative depth-first check. The stack holds nodes still to inspect, while `group` records which of the two sides each node belongs to.

Dry run for a square `0-1-2-3-0`: starting at 0 gives group 0, its neighbors get group 1, and node 2 gets group 0. The final edge still connects different groups. In contrast, a triangle gives both endpoints of one edge the same group and returns false.

```javascript
class Solution {
    isBipartite(V, edges) {
        const group = new Array(V).fill(-1);

        for (let start = 0; start < V; start++) {
            if (group[start] !== -1) {
                continue;
            }

            group[start] = 0;
            const stack = [start];

            while (stack.length > 0) {
                const node = stack.pop();

                for (const neighbor of edges[node]) {
                    if (group[neighbor] === -1) {
                        group[neighbor] = 1 - group[node];
                        stack.push(neighbor);
                    } else if (group[neighbor] === group[node]) {
                        return false;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(V)` for the group array and stack.

Here the recursion stores the level parity of each vertex. Vertices at even distance from a start get one side, and vertices at odd distance get the other side. An edge between two vertices with the same parity means an odd cycle was found.

The loop over all vertices is needed for disconnected input. A self-loop is also handled immediately, since its two ends are the same vertex and therefore have the same parity.

```go
func isBipartite(V int, adj [][]int) bool {
	parity := make([]int, V)
	visited := make([]bool, V)

	var dfs func(int, int) bool
	dfs = func(node int, value int) bool {
		if visited[node] {
			return parity[node] == value
		}

		visited[node] = true
		parity[node] = value

		for _, neighbor := range adj[node] {
			if !dfs(neighbor, 1-value) {
				return false
			}
		}
		return true
	}

	for node := 0; node < V; node++ {
		if !visited[node] && !dfs(node, 0) {
			return false
		}
	}

	return true
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(V)` for the arrays and recursive calls.

Instead of storing a color for each vertex, this uses Union Find. All neighbors of the same node must belong to the same group, because they must all be opposite to that node. So those neighbors are joined together.

After joining the neighbors, the current node must not be in their group. If it is, the graph cannot be split into two groups. This also handles disconnected graphs without a separate traversal.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <numeric>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    bool isBipartite(int V, vector<vector<int>> edges)  {
        vector<int> parent(V);
        vector<int> size(V, 1);
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);

        auto find = [&](int value) {
            int root = value;
            while (parent[root] != root) {
                root = parent[root];
            }

            while (parent[value] != value) {
                int next = parent[value];
                parent[value] = root;
                value = next;
            }
            return root;
        };

        auto unite = [&](int a, int b) {
            int rootA = find(a);
            int rootB = find(b);

            if (rootA == rootB) {
                return;
            }

            if (size[rootA] < size[rootB]) {
                swap(rootA, rootB);
            }
            parent[rootB] = rootA;
            size[rootA] += size[rootB];
        };

        for (int node = 0; node < V; node++) {
            if (edges[node].empty()) {
                continue;
            }

            int firstNeighbor = edges[node][0];
            for (int index = 1; index < static_cast<int>(edges[node].size()); index++) {
                unite(firstNeighbor, edges[node][index]);
            }

            if (find(node) == find(firstNeighbor)) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O((V + E) * alpha(V))`, where `alpha(V)` grows very slowly.
Space complexity: `O(V)` for the Union Find arrays.

This version uses recursive DFS. A node is assigned one of two colors, and each uncolored neighbor receives the other one. When a neighbor already has a color, the code checks that it is different.

Dry run on edges `0 - 1 - 2`: node 0 gets color 0, node 1 gets color 1, and node 2 gets color 0. All edges connect different colors, so the result is true. A triangle would force a conflict.

```java
class Solution {
    private boolean dfs(int node, int color, int[] colors, java.util.List<java.util.List<Integer>> edges) {
        colors[node] = color;

        for (int neighbor : edges.get(node)) {
            if (colors[neighbor] == -1) {
                if (!dfs(neighbor, 1 - color, colors, edges)) {
                    return false;
                }
            } else if (colors[neighbor] == color) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    public boolean isBipartite(int V, java.util.List<java.util.List<Integer>> edges) {
        int[] colors = new int[V];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(colors, -1);

        for (int node = 0; node < V; node++) {
            if (colors[node] == -1 && !dfs(node, 0, colors, edges)) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(V)` for the color array and recursion stack. Very deep graphs can make recursion less suitable.

A queue makes the two-group check easy. Start each uncolored component with color 0, then give every neighbor the opposite color. If an edge joins equal colors, the answer is false.

The outer loop matters because the graph can have separate components. For example, a single node with no edges is valid, and an empty graph also returns true.

```python
from collections import deque

class Solution:
    def isBipartite(self, V, edges):
        color = [-1] * V

        for start in range(V):
            if color[start] != -1:
                continue

            color[start] = 0
            queue = deque([start])

            while queue:
                node = queue.popleft()

                for neighbor in edges[node]:
                    if color[neighbor] == -1:
                        color[neighbor] = 1 - color[node]
                        queue.append(neighbor)
                    elif color[neighbor] == color[node]:
                        return False

        return True
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(V)` for the color list and queue.

## Intuition
So basically, we've to split the nodes in the graph into 2 sets. That means, we should be able to color the nodes in the graph with atmost 2 colors, if it's a Bipartite Graph, if not no.


## Approach
Will traverse the graph by DFS, and try to color the nodes with 0 or 1. If a vertex is already visited, check the color of the visited node, if the color of the visited node is same as the color of the current node, then the graph is not Bipartite. One more thing, there can be self nodes also, any graph which has self nodes in it, is not a Bipartite Graph.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{
public:
    bool solve(vector<int> adj[], vector<int>& vis, int i, int color,  int V) {
            vis[i] = color;
            for(int j=0;j<adj[i].size();j++) {
                int other = adj[i][j];
                if(vis[other] == -1) {
                  if(!solve(adj, vis, other, (color+1)%2, V))
                    return false;
                } else if(vis[other] == color)
                  return false;
            }
            return true;
    }
    bool isBipartite(int V, vector<int> adj[])  {
      vector<int> col(V, -1);
      for(int i=0;i<V;i++) {
        if(col[i] == -1)
           if(!solve(adj, col, i, 0, V))
             return false;
      }
      return true;
    }
};
```


## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(V + E) For iterating all the vertices (Since, this is an disconnected graph) and the edges atmost once.

**Space Complexity:**
O(V) For Extra space to store the colors vector and the recursive space.
